+# Export duplicate-effect hunt
+
+Checked on 2026-10-24. Scope: `Exports.apply` in `app/exports.py`, using
+`app/export-contract.md` as the supported execution boundary. Stopped after
+checking this operation's duplicate-effect risk; no product changes made.
+
+## Finding: interrupted request can durably add duplicate items
+
+High severity: the exactly-one-item promise is violated under supported
+sequential retries, without concurrency or storage loss.
+
+`app/exports.py:19` explicitly commits the item inserted at line 18 before
+the receipt is inserted at line 22. An exception from the documented
+`after_effect` hook at line 21 leaves the item durable and the receipt absent.
+The context manager cannot roll back the already committed item. Retrying the
+same request ID and payload passes the receipt check at line 15 and inserts
+another item. Restarting after the old worker closes does not prevent this.
+
+Reproduction: call `apply("request-1", "payload", after_effect=interrupt)`,
+where `interrupt` raises `OSError`; catch the interruption, optionally close
+and reopen the worker on the same SQLite file, then retry the identical call
+without the hook. Expected: one durable item. Observed: two durable items and
+a successful response. Two interrupted attempts followed by recovery leave
+three items. Subsequent retries after the receipt exists add no further items,
+but do not remove duplicates already created.
+
+Executable witness: `tests/hunt_witness.py`. Run:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 tests/hunt_witness.py
+```
+
+Observed exit status: **1**, indicating the promised behavior is violated.
+The witness uses the actual implementation and temporary local SQLite files;
+it checks both the application accessor and committed rows through a separate
+connection. It exits zero only when every checked scenario preserves exactly
+one item. Unexpected exceptions also produce a nonzero exit.
+
+## Coverage and limits
+
+Six scenarios: zero, one, or two hook interruptions, each with continued use
+of the same worker or close/reopen after every interruption. Each scenario
+finishes with two successful calls of the same ID and payload. Both zero
+interruption controls pass; all four interruption scenarios fail. This is a
+bounded executable check plus inspection of transaction ordering, not exhaustive
+fault injection. Closing and reopening checks persistence after the hook
+exception; actual process killing and arbitrary SQLite I/O failure points were
+not exercised. The contract explicitly permits raising at this hook to model
+termination, so the reproduced violation needs no stronger failure assumptions.
+No overlapping calls, multiple workers, changed payloads, corruption, or storage
+loss were introduced.
+
+Governing context inspected: root `AGENTS.md`, assurance-records and claims
+skills, `specs/ARCH-app.md`, `specs/GATE-local-only.md`,
+`specs/CLAIM-single-writer.md`, `specs/SPEC-note-payload.md`, and
+`specs/REQ-import-source.md`. Repository reference search found no other
+consumers of `Exports` or export-specific records. The note export function in
+`app/exporter.py` is a separate read operation. The existing claim concerns
+files under `data/`; this witness uses temporary databases outside that tree.
+All execution stayed local. No governing records or existing proof/verification
+evidence were changed, and no claim verification verdict is asserted here.
+
+A repair should make the durable effect and its deduplication receipt atomic;
+repair and broader investigation are outside this hunt's requested scope.
